    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d106">

                <pb facs="0288" n="252" xml:id="pg252"/>

                <head>106. Memorandum From Secretary of State Herter to President Eisenhower<anchor
                        n="0" xml:id="d106-f1"/></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1960-02-21">February 21, 1960</date>.</dateline></opener>
                <list subtype="subject" type="description">
                    <label>SUBJECT</label>
                    <item>European Economic Problems</item>
                </list>
                <p>I thought you would be interested in steps we have taken subsequent to the
                    economic meetings which Doug Dillon attended in Paris in January. I therefore
                    enclose a summary and status report of each of the three general topics
                    considered at those meetings: Trade Problems, Re-constitution of the OEEC, and
                    the Development Assistance Group.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#bold">Christian A. Herter</hi><anchor n="1"
                            xml:id="d106-f2"/></signed></closer>





                <xi:include href="d106-a1.xml" xpointer="d106-a1"/>
                <xi:include href="d106-a2.xml" xpointer="d106-a2"/>
                <xi:include href="d106-a3.xml" xpointer="d106-a3"/>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d106-f1" n="0" type="source" xml:id="d106-f1-content">
                <p>Source: Department of State, Central Files, 374.800/2–2160. Official Use Only. A
                    notation on the source text reads: “Hand carried by Sec. on SA trip 2/27/60.”
                    Eisenhower and Herter visited Brazil, Argentina, Chile, and Uruguay February
                    23-March 3.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d106-f2" n="1" xml:id="d106-f2-content">
                <p>Printed from a copy that bears this stamped signature.</p>
            </note>
